e reliable and allow sensible rebalancing of auxiliary objectives in subsequent iterations.", "validation_plan_suggested_by_judge": "Check that after the change no candidate reports quality_score > 1.0 or < 0.0 and that summary statistics for quality_score are within [0,1]. Monitor whether population-level improvements remain or improve (validator acceptance requires improvement over current accepted state). If sanitization preserves relative ordering of high-quality candidates, optimization should continue to improve.", "self_reported_confidence_uncalibrated": 0.8, "target_assessment": null}, "notes": [], "judge_raw_output": "The analyzer shows quality_score values violating their documented [0,1] range (max=1.9000, mean=1.8273) which breaks weighting/aggregation and trust in the optimization signal. The simplest safe fix is to sanitize the scorer outputs (clamp/validate) so downstream components see values in the declared range while we investigate the scorer implementation.\n\n<decision>\n{\n  \"failure_location\": \"implementation\",\n  \"root_cause_hypothesis\": \"The quality_score scorer is producing out-of-range or otherwise un-normalized outputs (observed max=1.9000, mean=1.8273 vs documented [0,1]). I am not certain whether this is a scorer bug or an aggregation/labeling error, but the immediate symptom is inconsistent scorer outputs.\",\n  \"intervention_target\": \"implementation\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Analyzer reports quality_score values exceeding the documented [0,1] range (max=1.9000, mean=1.8273). This will distort objective weighting and optimizer behavior. Sanitizing the quality_score outputs to the documented range is a low-risk, local fix that restores meaningful comparisons and weights without changing the objective definition.\",\n  \"proposed_change\": {\n    \"objective_name\": \"quality_score\",\n    \"action\": \"sanitize_outputs\",\n    \"valid_range\": [\n      0.0,\n      1.0\n    ]\n  },\n  \"expected_benefit\": \"All reported quality_score values will lie within [0,1], restoring consistent scale for aggregation and weighting. This should make optimizer decisions more reliable and allow sensible rebalancing of auxiliary objectives in subsequent iterations.\",\n  \"validation_plan\": \"Check that after the change no candidate reports quality_score > 1.0 or < 0.0 and that summary statistics for quality_score are within [0,1]. Monitor whether population-level improvements remain or improve (validator acceptance requires improvement over current accepted state). If sanitization preserves relative ordering of high-quality candidates, optimization should continue to improve.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.80\n}\n</decision>", "change_id": "1afed3924a02b855", "candidate_state": {"version": 0, "planner_guidance": null, "objective_ops": [], "implementation_patches": {"quality_score": {"action": "sanitize_outputs", "valid_range": [0.0, 1.0]}}, "workflow_params": {}}, "validation": {"seeds": [1000, 1001], "use_planner": false, "mode": "sequential", "max_stages": 3, "candidate_objectives": ["quality_score", "parameter_mean", "parameter_spread"], "baseline_objectives": ["quality_score", "parameter_mean", "parameter_spread"], "baseline": [1.4345182244550227e-07, 5.536094893286107e-07], "candidate": [0.04135635373984135, 0.007681927419472073], "baseline_mean": 3.485306558870565e-07, "candidate_mean": 0.024519140579656713, "delta": 0.024518792049000825, "min_improvement": 0.02, "candidate_constraint_violation": false, "details": {"baseline": [{"split": "validation", "top_k": 5, "top_k_by_true_quality": 3.5265781544208182e-06, "population_mean": 9.169680976067124e-07, "best": 5.390357366707214e-06, "n_invalid": 0}, {"split": "validation", "top_k": 5, "top_k_by_true_quality": 7.151987141382879e-06, "population_mean": 1.8052045943583469e-06, "best": 1.2660886369200155e-05, "n_invalid": 0}], "candidate": [{"split": "validation", "top_k": 5, "top_k_by_true_quality": 0.04709685107679812, "population_mean": 0.01901709377847529